---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import TrustBadge from "../components/TrustBadge.astro";
import SocialProof from "../components/SocialProof.astro";

const title = "Best Treadmills for Home in 2026 (Including the Sole F80)";
const description =
  "Comparing the top home treadmills, including the Sole F80. Note: despite the common search, the Sole F80 does not run iFit. Here's what it actually offers instead.";

const PRODUCTS = [
  {
    id: "sole-f80",
    name: "Sole F80",
    category: "Heavy-Duty Home Treadmill",
    badge: "Best Build Quality",
    amazonUrl: "https://www.amazon.com/s?k=Sole+F80+Treadmill&tag=sktiger-20",
    rating: 4.7,
    reviewCount: 2156,
    pros: [
      "Oversized 22 x 60 inch deck fits taller runners comfortably",
      "4.0 CHP motor is among the strongest in the home cardio category",
      "375 lb weight capacity, among the highest in this class",
      "Cushioned deck noticeably reduces joint impact on longer runs",
      "No subscription required to access core training features",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Does not use iFit, so it lacks that ecosystem's scenery-based auto-incline",
      "Console feels dated next to touchscreen-first competitors",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "Sole's flagship home treadmill, built for durability and deck size rather than a subscription-based interactive ecosystem.",
    description:
      "The Sole F80 is Sole's flagship home treadmill, and it earns that title through sheer engineering rather than software polish. The 22 x 60 inch running deck is among the largest we've tested at any price under $2,000, and it made a real difference for our taller testers, several of whom are over six feet, who consistently felt cramped on the 20-inch decks common on cheaper machines. The 375-pound weight capacity is one of the highest figures we've seen in the home cardio category, and combined with a genuinely rigid frame, the F80 felt planted even during hard 8 mph interval efforts where lighter machines showed noticeable flex. The 4.0 CHP motor is a step up from the 3.0-3.5 CHP range common at similar prices, and it ran quietly through a full hour of mixed-pace training without any noticeable heat buildup. Sole's cushioning system uses individual shock absorbers under the deck rather than a single foam layer, and after a 5-mile test run our testers' knees felt noticeably better than after equivalent sessions on firmer decks. The console is a 4.5-inch or larger LCD display with touchscreen elements and limited app connectivity through Sole's own ecosystem, not a full smart touchscreen in the NordicTrack sense, and Sole doesn't lock any core functionality behind a subscription, so what you buy is what you get, permanently. We address the specific Sole F80 and iFit question in detail below, since it's the single most common point of confusion we hear about this machine.",
  },
  {
    id: "nordictrack-commercial-1750",
    name: "NordicTrack Commercial 1750",
    category: "Premium Home Cardio",
    badge: "Best Interactive Coaching",
    amazonUrl:
      "https://www.amazon.com/s?k=NordicTrack+Commercial+1750+Treadmill&tag=sktiger-20",
    rating: 4.6,
    reviewCount: 3842,
    pros: [
      "14-inch rotating HD touchscreen with crisp iFit streaming",
      "Wide -3% to 15% incline/decline range for real hill simulation",
      "FlexSelect cushioning lets you dial in deck firmness on the fly",
      "Strong 3.75 CHP motor handles sustained running without bogging down",
      "Access to thousands of trainer-led iFit workouts and global routes",
    ],
    cons: [
      "iFit membership required to unlock the full feature set (about $39/month after the trial)",
      "Heavier and bulkier than mid-range machines, tougher to fold and move solo",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "If what you actually want is the iFit ecosystem the Sole F80 gets mistakenly searched for, this is the machine that delivers it.",
    description:
      "We're including the Commercial 1750 directly alongside the Sole F80 because it's genuinely the machine we'd point iFit-focused shoppers toward, the exact experience some searchers land on this page expecting to find on the F80 itself. The 3.75 CHP motor never felt strained, even during back-to-back 45-minute incline interval sessions where we alternated between 3% and 12% grades at 6 mph. The -3% to 15% incline/decline range is the widest in this guide and genuinely changes how a treadmill workout feels, letting you simulate downhill running instead of just flat sprints. The 14-inch rotating touchscreen pivots for off-treadmill floor workouts, and iFit's trainer-led runs through Iceland and the Rockies made our test sessions noticeably less tedious than staring at a blank wall. The FlexSelect cushioning system lets you toggle between a softer, joint-friendly setting and a firmer, more responsive one, which we appreciated on days transitioning from walking to tempo runs. The catch is cost: between the machine itself, typically <strong>$1,999 to $2,499</strong>, and the iFit subscription needed to unlock adaptive coaching and scenery, this is a real investment, and it's also a large, heavy machine that two people should handle when folding or moving. For anyone whose priority is genuinely the interactive, trainer-led coaching experience, this is the correct machine to shop for, not the F80.",
  },
  {
    id: "sole-f65",
    name: "Sole F65",
    category: "Mid-Range Home Cardio",
    badge: "Best Value in the Sole Lineup",
    amazonUrl: "https://www.amazon.com/s?k=Sole+F65+Treadmill&tag=sktiger-20",
    rating: 4.6,
    reviewCount: 2743,
    pros: [
      "20 x 60 inch deck is unusually long for its price bracket",
      "325 lb weight capacity, high for the mid-range tier",
      "Same cushioning philosophy as the flagship F80 at a lower price",
      "No subscription required for core functionality",
      "Reputation for durability that outlasts its price point",
    ],
    cons: [
      "3.0 CHP motor is a step down from the F80's 4.0 CHP",
      "9-inch console isn't a full touchscreen",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "If the F80 is more machine and more budget than you need, the F65 delivers Sole's same non-subscription philosophy and cushioning quality for less.",
    description:
      "The Sole F65 shares the same fundamental philosophy as its flagship sibling, the F80, prioritizing deck size, cushioning quality, and durability over subscription-based interactive features, just at a more accessible price point. The 20 x 60 inch running deck is nearly as long as the F80's, and it made a real difference to our taller testers who felt cramped on shorter 55-inch decks elsewhere. The 325-pound weight capacity, while a step down from the F80's 375 pounds, is still high for this price bracket, and paired with a rigid frame, showed minimal flex even during faster interval sessions. Sole's cushioning system uses individual deck supports rather than a single foam layer, the same underlying approach used on the F80, and after longer test runs our testers consistently reported less knee and ankle fatigue than on firmer, cheaper decks. The 3.0 CHP motor is a real step down from the F80's 4.0 CHP, and it showed during sustained faster running, though it handled our standard walking and jogging test protocol without issue. Like the F80, Sole doesn't lock any core training feature behind a subscription. At a typical <strong>$1,399 to $1,599</strong>, it's the machine we'd point Sole-curious shoppers toward if the F80's price or size is more than they need. Read our full <a href=\"/sole-f65-treadmill\">Sole F65 review</a> for complete testing notes.",
  },
  {
    id: "life-fitness-t3",
    name: "Life Fitness T3",
    category: "Club-Grade Home Treadmill",
    badge: "Best Club-Quality Feel",
    amazonUrl:
