          <SocialProof users="7.3k" />
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- Table of Contents -->
      <div class="my-10 bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
        <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-gray-500 mb-4">
          On This Page
        </p>
        <ol class="space-y-2 list-none p-0 m-0">
          <li>
            <a
              href="#sole-f80-ifit"
              class="text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium"
              >1. Sole F80 and iFit: Clearing Up the Confusion</a
            >
          </li>
          <li>
            <a
              href="#what-f80-offers"
              class="text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium"
              >2. What the Sole F80 Actually Offers Instead</a
            >
          </li>
          <li>
            <a
              href="#top-picks"
              class="text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium"
              >3. The Best Home Treadmills Compared</a
            >
          </li>
          <li>
            <a
              href="#subscription-vs-not"
              class="text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium"
              >4. Subscription Ecosystem vs. No-Subscription: Which Fits You?</a
            >
          </li>
          <li>
            <a
              href="#choosing"
              class="text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium"
              >5. How to Choose Between These Five</a
            >
          </li>
          <li>
            <a href="#faq" class="text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium"
              >6. Frequently Asked Questions</a
            >
          </li>
        </ol>
      </div>

      <div class="prose prose-lg max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <!-- Lede -->
        <p class="italic text-xl border-l-4 border-[#FF5A1F] bg-gray-50 pl-6 pr-4 py-4 rounded-r-xl not-prose text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
          If you searched for "Sole F80 iFit" and landed here, we want to
          answer your actual question before anything else: the Sole F80
          does not run iFit. It never has. It uses Sole's own console and
          an optional Sole+ app, a completely separate ecosystem from
          NordicTrack and ProForm's iFit platform. That mix-up is common
          enough that we're addressing it directly and clearly before we
          get into anything else, because we'd rather correct the record
          than let a false assumption shape your buying decision.
        </p>

        <!-- SECTION 1 -->
        <h2 id="sole-f80-ifit" class="scroll-mt-24">
          Sole F80 and iFit: Clearing Up the Confusion
        </h2>
        <p>
          We see this search combination often enough that it's worth
          explaining where the confusion likely comes from. iFit is the
          interactive training platform built into NordicTrack and
          ProForm machines, both owned by the same parent company, and
          it's become almost synonymous with "smart treadmill" in a lot of
          shoppers' minds because of how heavily it's marketed. Sole is a
          completely separate company with its own console software, and
          the F80, despite being one of the most popular and highly
          reviewed home treadmills on the market, was never built around
          iFit and has no plans to be.
        </p>
        <p>
          We suspect part of the confusion also comes from how often the
          Sole F80 and NordicTrack's iFit-compatible machines get compared
          directly in buying guides and forums, since they compete for
          many of the same shoppers at a similar price point. It's an easy
          mental slip to assume a frequently-compared machine shares a
          feature that's actually specific to its competitor, especially
          when review content doesn't always spell out the distinction
          clearly. We're spelling it out here specifically because we
          think shoppers deserve a direct, unambiguous answer rather than
          having to piece it together from scattered forum posts.
        </p>
        <p>
          There's also a broader pattern worth naming: treadmill shopping
          in general involves a dense thicket of brand names, console
          types, and app ecosystems that don't map cleanly onto each
          other, and it's genuinely easy to conflate one company's
          feature with another's, especially when comparing machines
          across price points where you'd reasonably expect similar
          capabilities. NordicTrack, ProForm, Sole, Horizon, Life Fitness,
          and Matrix all take meaningfully different approaches to
          console software, and none of it is standardized in a way that
          lets you assume features carry over from one brand to the
          next just because two machines look similar in photos or sit
          at a similar price. We'd encourage treating every "smart"
          feature claim as brand-specific until verified, rather than
          assuming any given software capability applies universally
          across the category.
        </p>

        <div class="not-prose bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8 my-8 border-2 border-[#5AA9FF]">
          <p class="font-black text-[#5AA9FF] uppercase tracking-widest text-xs mb-3">
            Direct Answer
          </p>
          <p class="text-white text-lg leading-relaxed">
            Despite the common search for "Sole F80 iFit," the Sole F80
            does not run iFit — it uses Sole's own console and optional
            Sole+ app. If you specifically want iFit's trainer-led,
            scenery-matched coaching, look at a NordicTrack model instead,
            like the Commercial 1750 covered later in this guide.
          </p>
        </div>

        <p>
          To be clear about what this means practically: the F80 will not
          stream iFit's trainer-led workout videos, it will not
          auto-adjust incline to match an instructor's on-screen terrain,
          and it will not sync with an iFit account or membership in any
          way. None of that is a flaw in the F80 exactly, it's simply a
          different product philosophy, one built around durable hardware
          and a self-contained console rather than a subscription-based
          content ecosystem. Whether that's the right fit for you depends
          entirely on what you were actually hoping to get out of the
          "iFit" part of your search.
        </p>

        <!-- SECTION 2 -->
        <h2 id="what-f80-offers" class="scroll-mt-24">
          What the Sole F80 Actually Offers Instead
        </h2>
        <p>
          Now that the misconception is cleared up, let's talk about what
          the F80 does deliver, because it remains one of the best-built
          home treadmills we've tested, on its own terms. The console is
          a 4.5-inch or larger LCD display with touchscreen elements,
          smaller and simpler than NordicTrack's large rotating
          touchscreens, but functional for navigating built-in workout
          programs, adjusting speed and incline, and viewing your
          real-time stats.
        </p>
        <p>
          Connectivity comes through Bluetooth and the optional Sole+ app,
          which lets you track workout history, log sessions over time,
          and in some cases sync with select third-party fitness apps.
          This is a genuinely more limited software experience than
          iFit's trainer-led video library and automatic terrain
          matching, and we want to be upfront about that rather than
          overselling it. What you get in exchange is a treadmill that
          works fully and permanently without any subscription
          requirement, ever, for its core training functions.
        </p>
        <p>
          Where the F80 spends its engineering budget instead of software
          is hardware: the oversized 22 x 60 inch deck, the 375-pound
          weight capacity, the 4.0 CHP motor, and Sole's individual
          shock-absorber cushioning system. In our testing, these
          hardware advantages are what set the F80 apart from competitors
          at a similar price, not any interactive coaching feature. For
          runners who prioritize deck size, motor strength, and long-term
          durability over trainer-led video content, this is a completely
          reasonable, even preferable, trade-off.
        </p>
        <p>
          It's worth walking through the F80's core specs in a bit more
          detail, since they're the actual reason this machine has such a
          strong reputation independent of any software conversation. The
          22 x 60 inch deck is longer and wider than the 20 x 55 inch
          decks common on competing machines at similar price points, and
          that extra size matters most for taller runners and anyone with
          a longer natural stride at faster paces, where a narrower or
          shorter deck starts to feel genuinely confining rather than
          just slightly snug. The 375-pound weight capacity is among the
          highest we've measured in the home treadmill category outside
          of true commercial equipment, giving heavier users a real
          safety buffer rather than running close to a machine's stated
          limit.
        </p>
        <p>
          The 4.0 CHP motor is another area where the F80 distinguishes
          itself from typical home-tier competitors, most of which sit in
          the 3.0 to 3.5 CHP range. During extended sustained-running
          test sessions, that extra motor headroom translated to less
          audible strain and less perceptible heat buildup in the motor
          housing compared to smaller motors pushed to similar speeds.
          Sole's cushioning system, built around individual shock
          absorbers positioned under the deck rather than a single sheet
          of foam, is engineered to soften impact more precisely at each
          footstrike location rather than relying on uniform padding
          across the whole deck, which is part of why testers with joint
          sensitivity consistently rated it among the more comfortable
          decks we've tested at any price.
        </p>

