        <!-- SECTION 4 -->
        <h2 id="subscription-vs-not" class="scroll-mt-24">
          Subscription Ecosystem vs. No-Subscription: Which Fits You?
        </h2>
        <p>
          The F80-versus-iFit question above is really a specific case of
          a broader decision every home treadmill shopper faces: do you
          want a machine built around an ongoing content subscription, or
          one that delivers its full value the day you buy it and every
          day after, with no recurring bill?
        </p>
        <p>
          The subscription model, which iFit represents most visibly in
          this category, trades a lower relationship to hardware cost for
          an ongoing monthly fee, typically around <strong>$39/month</strong>
          after an included trial. In exchange, you get a large, actively
          updated library of trainer-led workouts, automatic
          terrain-matching incline adjustments, and a more entertainment-
          forward experience that some users find meaningfully more
          motivating than a plain console. Over several years, though,
          that subscription cost compounds; two or three years of iFit
          membership can exceed a thousand dollars, worth weighing
          against the hardware price itself.
        </p>
        <p>
          The no-subscription model, which Sole and Life Fitness both
          represent in this guide, asks for a higher relative hardware
          investment upfront but delivers everything the console can do
          indefinitely, with no future bills. What you give up is the
          expanding content library and automated terrain-matching that
          subscription platforms offer. For buyers who mainly want a
          reliable, durable treadmill for self-directed walking or
          running, that trade-off often makes sense. For buyers who
          specifically value structured, trainer-led coaching to stay
          motivated, it may not.
        </p>
        <p>
          Neither approach is universally better, and we've tried to make
          that clear throughout this guide rather than pushing you toward
          one philosophy. What matters is being honest about which one
          actually fits how you'll use the machine, which is exactly the
          decision that gets muddled when a shopper assumes a
          non-subscription machine like the F80 includes a subscription
          feature it was never built around.
        </p>

        <div class="not-prose bg-amber-50 border border-amber-200 rounded-2xl p-6 my-8">
